00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.110
So a quick background
on Cloud Spanner.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.100
It's internally used
within Google as Spanner.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:37.040
It is our transactional
record of state

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.660
for hundreds of applications.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:42.080
And it was born not sort
of in the beginning.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.080
In the beginning, Google was
busy solving how to index

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.070
the internet and sort of find
all of these massive patterns

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:50.921
and provide meaningful search.

00:01:50.921 --> 00:01:52.670
And it wasn't something
that was initially

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.670
tackled until it became
critical that we did tackle it

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.580
based on the size of
some of our businesses.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.680
But when we did, we
realized a lot of challenges

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.300
that we were
overcoming are faced

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.560
by a lot of organizations.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.960
How do you, at scale,
maintain a database

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.382
with high reliability,
high durability,

00:02:08.382 --> 00:02:10.340
transactional throughput,
something that can be

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.676
used in financial transactions?

00:02:12.676 --> 00:02:14.800
And so Cloud Spanner's the
externalization of that.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.540
We realized a lot of customers
have the same challenges

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.990
and have gone down different
paths to try to solve it.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:28.310
So if you are coming from that
sort of, hey, broad use of data

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.380
but have had to make
trade-offs in the past, had

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.780
to decide on what you're
willing to give up,

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.800
hopefully, this talk will
be applicable to you.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.010
It's enterprise-grade.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.700
Google uses it to run a
multibillion dollar enterprise.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.280
We have other customers
using it, as well.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.607
But really, it's designed,
with a high SLA in mind,

00:02:46.607 --> 00:02:47.690
to be strictly consistent.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:49.396
It is ACID compliant.

00:02:49.396 --> 00:02:50.270
And it's distributed.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.480
It's the thing that a
lot of organizations

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:56.630
try to solve with
distributed architectures,

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:00.560
with the multi-server
approach, with multi-nodes,

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.590
with a lot of interconnect and
a lot of distributed storage.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:04.730
It is all that.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.200
But instead, it's just a
database behind an API.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.800
It gives you all that
distributed nature,

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.620
but you don't really have to
do much to get through to it.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:14.120
It's relational in nature.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.300
So relational data is
like the easiest thing

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.000
to get your head around if
you've been around databases.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.080
It's been around for
a very long time.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.410
It's something anybody
can sort of reason

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.840
about when they go from
one team to another.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.954
Within Google, we value
developer time considerably.

00:03:29.954 --> 00:03:31.370
And it doesn't
make a lot of sense

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:33.500
to have developers move
from one team to another

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.916
and sort of have to say, well,
what storage are you using?

00:03:35.916 --> 00:03:37.010
How are you using it?

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:38.630
What are their
canonical queries?

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:41.780
Everybody sort of gets SQL
and gets the relational model,

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:42.630
pretty well.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.840
And so there's a very
little, low friction point

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.540
to move saliently between teams
and say, oh, yeah, I know.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.081
You're using Spanner
at the back end?

00:03:49.081 --> 00:03:50.480
I can get going on that.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.040
And it's horizontally scalable.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.760
It's not much.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.090
It's not this sort of
heavy-handed, you can scale up,

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.710
but you can't scale down.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.060
And it's not where you have
to make a capacity request.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.000
That's where kind of
poke an API and you

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.780
get a lot more transactional
throughput through to it.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.220
To spin it up,
it's pretty simple.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:12.210
You name the instance, choose a
region, pick a number of nodes,

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.555
and hit Create.

00:04:14.555 --> 00:04:16.430
I just wanted to show
that, because I thought

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:17.840
it was a pretty cool animation.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:21.709
I didn't make it.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:24.180
So really, you just have
to reason about two things,

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.970
and then I'll turn
it over to Niel.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.840
And naming in computer
science is everything, right?

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:33.230
So you have to name it.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:35.510
But then you have to
pick how many nodes.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:37.400
Nodes is really just
your unit of capacity.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.260
It is the most
sensitive thing to cost.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:40.920
You pick how many
nodes you need.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.771
And we'll go through what each
node gives you in a little bit.

00:04:43.771 --> 00:04:45.020
And then where do you want it?

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.061
So how much capacity do
you want and where do you

00:04:47.061 --> 00:04:48.119
want it, which region?

00:04:48.119 --> 00:04:49.160
Do you want multi-region?

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.580
The product comes in a lot
of different varieties.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.719
And the last thing
is take your code,

00:04:54.719 --> 00:04:56.510
merge it together with
our client libraries

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:59.990
or speak SQL to us, and
then you're ready to go.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:01.790
We understand DDL as a database.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.280
So things that you're used
to doing in other databases

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:05.390
are pretty easy to use.

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:07.200
Similar to BigQuery
and other products,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.500
there is a built-in
query window.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:12.160
So that's the basic.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.130
What I described takes
longer than it actually

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.130
does to actually get
started with Cloud Spanner.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.720
So now let's talk
about migrations,

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.491
how we're going to talk about
the rest of the programs we're

00:05:20.491 --> 00:05:22.490
going to walk through in
terms of the migration,

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:23.570
practical examples.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:27.004
Neal's going to
talk through that.

00:05:27.004 --> 00:05:28.170
NIEL MARKWICK: So, hi there.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.169
My name's Niel Markwick.

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:31.150
I'm a solution
architect from Belgium.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:32.970
And so in this
section, I'm going

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.685
to give a general framework
for migrations, giving you

00:05:35.685 --> 00:05:37.684
a high level plan of
action for migrating

00:05:37.684 --> 00:05:39.850
your application from the
existing database to Cloud

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:41.220
Spanner.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.485
So the first step,
you need to isolate

00:05:43.485 --> 00:05:45.120
all your database access code.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.078
If you've got it distributed
in various modules

00:05:47.078 --> 00:05:49.800
in your application,
including any lines of code,

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.086
it's best to isolate that
all into a separate module.

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:54.960
And that will be the
one place where you have

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.966
all your database access code.

00:05:57.966 --> 00:05:59.340
And be sure your
development team

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.600
only adds new code
in that module,

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.022
so you know exactly what
code talks to the database

00:06:04.022 --> 00:06:06.480
and exactly what code needs be
changed during the migration

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:07.860
process.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.840
In general, this is
a very good practice.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.960
Because it gives you one single
module to test your database

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:14.130
interactions.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.230
And similarly, it
enables your unit testing

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.120
of your application.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.850
You only need one single
model module to mock out.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.530
You don't need to
have a test database.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.655
You don't need to have some
weird and wonderful way

00:06:24.655 --> 00:06:26.822
of creating some
data that takes care

00:06:26.822 --> 00:06:28.530
of some weird, wonderful
edge conditions.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.050
You can mock all that out
and supply this bad data

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.841
to the rest of your
application to test how

00:06:32.841 --> 00:06:34.490
your application handles it.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.600
It makes it a lot easier
to find and analyze

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.870
any complex SQL queries you've
got in your application.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:43.590
And it gives you a single
point to add metrics and alerts

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:46.380
for monitoring your
database access.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.642
For instance, you could add
an alert for query latency,

00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:51.600
so that you know when
your queries are starting

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.240
to get a bit slow, it's time
to resize your database,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.590
add more power.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:58.597
Or you see which particular
queries are slow,

00:06:58.597 --> 00:07:01.180
so you don't need to dive deeper
to find out why they're slow.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.060
Maybe optimize the
query a bit better,

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.865
change the way things
are read, perhaps.

00:07:04.865 --> 00:07:06.720
It also allows you,
with the metrics,

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.040
to check the frequency of
reads to the tables, which

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.110
may give you an indication
of whether you can add

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:15.504
some client-side caching
to your database layer,

00:07:15.504 --> 00:07:17.670
thus giving a performance
boost to your application,

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:19.320
because it no longer needs
to read from the database.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.790
It can get it from
the local cache.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:25.770
And of course, failure alerts,
so any read-write failures,

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.900
any inconsistency
problems, you got

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.750
to place to alert it and
catch the problem before it

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.370
starts getting really bad.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.260
And, as I said, you've
got a single isolated

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.150
module for migration.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.110
So your next step is to add
the code to that module that

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:41.280
reads and writes to Spanner.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.740
Sounds simple.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:46.940
But we'll look into more
details about that later.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:48.990
You'll be reading and
writing at the same time

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:51.930
as reading or writing from
the original database.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:56.250
You would include capabilities,
in your application, that

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.440
are not available in Spanner.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.200
Again, more on that later.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.157
And the code would be
reading to and writing

00:08:03.157 --> 00:08:04.740
from both, at the
same time, but you'd

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.770
have one database as
your source of truth.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:07.620
At the beginning,
of course, it would

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:08.895
be your original database.

00:08:08.895 --> 00:08:10.770
And once you've fully
migrated your data over

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.561
and you're sure there's
no inconsistencies,

00:08:12.561 --> 00:08:16.290
you can switch to Cloud Spanner
as your source of truth.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:20.690
So when you're writing to
a database, to Spanner,

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:22.140
you should update
the entire row.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:23.490
You shouldn't just
update the columns.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.906
And that way, you're ensuring
that the data you're writing

00:08:25.906 --> 00:08:29.060
to Spanner is fully up to date.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.560
On reads, you can read
from both databases

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.169
and compare any
inconsistencies and report

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:35.520
those inconsistencies.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:37.230
Again, this is to
gain confidence

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.110
that your migration is
happening correctly.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.880
And once all your
data is imported

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:43.421
and everything is
synchronized, there

00:08:43.421 --> 00:08:45.750
should be no inconsistency,
so no more failures.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:47.250
One other thing you
should be doing,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.010
if you do deletions of
rows in your database,

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:51.740
you should keep a log of these.

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:54.197
When you are
importing data, rows

00:08:54.197 --> 00:08:55.530
might have already been deleted.

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:56.820
So you don't want
to be recreating

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:58.778
these rows you've already
deleted whilst you're

00:08:58.778 --> 00:08:59.760
doing your import.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.260
Keeping a log of
the deletions will

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:03.426
allow you to check whether
this row has been deleted

00:09:03.426 --> 00:09:04.700
when you're re-importing it.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.839
And finally, one really useful
thing is commit timestamps.

00:09:07.839 --> 00:09:09.380
Some databases do
this automatically.

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:11.550
In Spanner, it's an option.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:14.034
You have a column, which
is the timestamp when

00:09:14.034 --> 00:09:15.200
that row has been committed.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.890
And you can use this to know
which is the latest data

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:19.890
version, whether it's the
data you are importing

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.723
or it's the data that's
already been written

00:09:21.723 --> 00:09:23.222
by your application to Spanner.

00:09:23.222 --> 00:09:24.930
And also, this module--
one other thing--

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:28.110
allows you to canary the
use of your new database.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:30.976
You switch the source's
truth partially.

00:09:30.976 --> 00:09:32.850
You can still have some
application instances

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:34.320
talking to Spanner
and some talking

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:35.800
to the original database.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:39.884
So if you catch any problems
early, you can rollback.

00:09:39.884 --> 00:09:41.300
So how would you
actually migrate?

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.080
Well, a simple method.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:45.620
You convert your database
schema to Cloud Spanner schema

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:46.940
and create your database.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.210
You shut down your application.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.890
You do your export and import
whilst your database is not

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.110
being written to, so
it's completely stable.

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:56.240
And you start it up again.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:01.170
This is not an ideal
path for several reasons.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.350
Firstly, there's no rollback.

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:05.212
Your application, if
you start noticing,

00:10:05.212 --> 00:10:07.170
after a couple of days,
things are going wrong,

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.128
some data is getting
corrupted, what do you do?

00:10:09.128 --> 00:10:12.140
You can't rollback, because you
got a couple of days' new data.

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:14.300
You can't fix the data.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:15.917
You just might start panicking.

00:10:15.917 --> 00:10:18.500
If you rollback, you've lost the
data, and you have a problem.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:21.554
And secondly, your
application is down.

00:10:21.554 --> 00:10:22.970
If you are moving
to Spanner, it's

00:10:22.970 --> 00:10:25.190
presumably because you
want to take advantage

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:27.530
of the scalability
and the availability,

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:29.750
so you wouldn't want
the downtime associated

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.562
with an export and an
import of a large data set.

00:10:32.562 --> 00:10:33.270
So what do we do?

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:35.960
We want a live
migration process.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:37.820
Once again, you start
with the same way.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:42.230
You create your database
on Cloud Spanner,

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:45.200
and you export and import
from your original database

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:46.070
to Spanner.

00:10:46.070 --> 00:10:48.320
But this time, you let the
application stay running.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.445
Now, of course, whilst your
application is running,

00:10:50.445 --> 00:10:52.440
your exported data is
already out of date.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:54.064
But that's not a
problem, because we'll

00:10:54.064 --> 00:10:57.120
see you write to both.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.540
You keep the original database,
as your source of truth,

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:01.430
and continue updating
rows in Spanner.

00:11:01.430 --> 00:11:03.560
There will still be some
instance inconsistencies,

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.476
but that's why your
original database is still

00:11:05.476 --> 00:11:07.760
the source of truth.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.710
Then, whilst that's
going on, you

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:13.130
do another import and export
of any changes that have

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:15.380
occurred since the last export.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.780
Now this will bring
your data in sync

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:20.660
provided that you only write
rows that haven't already

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.770
been updated.

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:25.130
So you also, at this
point, replay any deletions

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.060
that have occurred.

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:29.420
And when this is
completed, it should

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:32.360
be that your Cloud Spanner
database is fully in sync

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.370
with your original database.

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:37.140
And the monitoring that you've
added in your database access

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:39.640
layer will not be showing any
more inconsistencies, will not

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.077
be showing any more errors.

00:11:41.077 --> 00:11:43.410
You can repeat this last step
as many times as you want.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.618
And at this stage, you can
also run a massive compare

00:11:45.618 --> 00:11:49.330
to see if everything is in sync.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:51.560
Finally, when you are
convinced everything is good--

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:52.850
not finally, sorry.

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:55.552
You switch to be this Cloud
Spanner is the source of truth.

00:11:55.552 --> 00:11:57.010
Again, like I
mentioned before, you

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:58.468
can do this as a
canarying process.

00:11:58.468 --> 00:12:01.130
You can partially switch
some application instances

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:04.095
or a percentage of the traffic,
and then monitor things,

00:12:04.095 --> 00:12:05.720
see if everything is
good and gradually

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:07.095
increase the
percentages which is

00:12:07.095 --> 00:12:08.900
being written to Cloud Spanner.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:11.810
And if things go wrong, you
can immediately rollback.

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:14.360
Because your original database
is still being written,

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.460
you've got a perfect
rollback position to go to.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:17.510
You won't lose any data.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.100
Everything's fine.

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:20.840
But if everything
has gone forward,

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:23.240
everything is working well,
and everything's fixed,

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:24.806
then you can turn
off the code that

00:12:24.806 --> 00:12:26.180
writes to your
original database,

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:29.030
turn off your original
database, shut it down,

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:31.130
and you're fully
running on Spanner.

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.920
And at this point, of course,
you have your launch party.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:41.030
And you're back to Sami, now,
who will talk about the example

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:43.679
from migrating from a NoSQL
database to Cloud Spanner.

00:12:43.679 --> 00:12:45.720
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN: I really
wanted to have a party.

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:46.760
NIEL MARKWICK: Yeah.

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:47.260
Later.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:47.520
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN: Later?

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:48.392
OK.

00:12:48.392 --> 00:12:50.100
NIEL MARKWICK: After
we finish this talk.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.880
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN: Let's see.

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:54.040
Big button.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:58.300
Migrating from NoSQL--
so the obvious thing

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:02.424
here is that Spanner is
not a NoSQL database.

00:13:02.424 --> 00:13:04.090
But we have a lot of
customers and a lot

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:07.780
of interest for customers
coming to us and saying,

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:11.080
hey, I made these design
choices in my application

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.120
stack a while ago,
where I couldn't

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:16.930
scale in a relational database,
a traditional relational

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:18.140
database very easily.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:20.890
And so I had to decide what
I was going to give up.

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:23.710
And most of the
time, organizations

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:26.359
chose to give up
transactional consistency.

00:13:26.359 --> 00:13:28.900
Hey, let's give up transactional
consistency, because then we

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:31.150
can shard, really easily.

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:32.772
Or they decided,
you know what, we'll

00:13:32.772 --> 00:13:34.480
maintain a relational
database, but we'll

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:37.930
employ some sort of shim,
some sort of sharding shim,

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:40.570
and so we're not really
getting true transactions

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:42.460
across the entire
database in one context,

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:45.460
but we'll have to sort of
manipulate it and introduce

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:46.990
some plumbing in between.

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:48.490
So really, the
point of this slide

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:51.230
is that you can have your
cake and eat it, too.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:52.630
Spanner takes care of that.

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:57.140
It takes care of that ongoing,
true horizontal scalability,

00:13:57.140 --> 00:13:59.980
while giving you
transactional consistency,

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:06.052
while giving you the ability
to keep a very high up time.

00:14:06.052 --> 00:14:08.260
And then it gets you back
to a very simplistic model,

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:10.593
where you're not having to
deal with, is this key value,

00:14:10.593 --> 00:14:11.590
is it document?

00:14:11.590 --> 00:14:13.450
What is the underlying storage?

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:18.190
This is very simple to
understand SQL semantics.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:21.130
So when does it apply?

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:22.446
So when would you do this?

00:14:22.446 --> 00:14:24.070
You wouldn't do this
for something that

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:25.670
is highly volatile in nature.

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:27.490
So if you're just
keeping track of the key,

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:30.100
and then you have an arbitrary
number of attributes associated

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:35.170
with that, and it's really
hard for you to get a grasp of,

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:37.610
is there a well-known consistent
pattern to the attributes

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:40.390
we're keeping track of, with the
thought that those attributes

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:42.306
would translate to
columns, then this wouldn't

00:14:42.306 --> 00:14:43.720
be a pattern for you to follow.

00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:46.120
This would be, really, if
you're like, generally,

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:49.300
we're using it as a way to keep
track of certain attributes.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:51.370
The collection or the
set of those attributes

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:52.900
is relatively static.

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:55.360
Maybe some attributes add,
here or there, but that's OK.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:57.610
We can do schema
changes within Spanner.

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:00.200
But it's not too varied.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:01.930
It's not too all over the place.

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:03.070
Reasonably high volume--

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:04.720
Spanner is a running
service, which

00:15:04.720 --> 00:15:07.270
means you instantiate a certain
amount of capacity to it.

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:09.250
So if you're only,
say, transacting

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:11.380
with the database
at a trickle volume,

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:13.450
it's just some sort of
light lookup or something,

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:14.150
that's not it.

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:16.358
The use cases we're seeing
are folks that are saying,

00:15:16.358 --> 00:15:19.480
I'm wholesale running a
massively high transactional

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:25.030
throughput beyond
just a light workload.

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:27.562
The source database should
have a pretty easy way--

00:15:27.562 --> 00:15:29.020
I think most
databases, today, will

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:32.440
have a pretty easy way to
import and export data,

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:36.255
so much like what
Niel was mentioning--

00:15:36.255 --> 00:15:39.070
not yet-- much
like what Niel was

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:44.450
mentioning in the migration
general framework.

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:46.330
And the last thing
is it's a real plus

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.820
if the source system has
some sort of mechanism

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:50.654
to do the equivalent
of change data capture.

00:15:50.654 --> 00:15:52.653
If you can sort of know
what's coming in and out

00:15:52.653 --> 00:15:54.210
of that database, it's great.

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:57.010
Some source examples, that
we're seeing a lot of,

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.430
are folks that are running
their own Mongo infrastructure,

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:02.500
own Cassandra infrastructure,
that also want to get out

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:07.150
of that pattern, but
also folks coming

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:11.980
from more traditional or
more service offerings,

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:13.162
like DynamoDB.

00:16:13.162 --> 00:16:14.620
So we're going to
pick one of these

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:15.970
to walk through a migration.

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:18.680
Anybody want to take a guess?

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:19.760
Right, so DynamoDB.

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.949
So it's pretty straightforward.

00:16:25.949 --> 00:16:27.240
It's actually not that complex.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:29.910
DynamoDB is a single
table structure.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:31.830
It's not many tables
related to one.

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:33.630
It has some nice,
interesting similarities

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.820
with regards to Cloud
Spanner in terms of the way

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.710
keys are laid out.

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:38.460
But it is a table.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:40.920
And we're going to go through
a straightforward path.

00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:42.330
This kind of
aligns, very nicely,

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:43.871
to what Niel was
talking about, where

00:16:43.871 --> 00:16:45.870
we're going to have a
bulk loading path that's

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:50.031
going to come in through
certain export features.

00:16:50.031 --> 00:16:52.530
And then we'll have our trickle
feeding, our streaming path.

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:56.910
We'll collate those onto the
blue icons or Google Cloud.

00:16:56.910 --> 00:16:59.780
We'll basically coalesce
those onto the cloud side,

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:02.310
bring those together, using
a technology on our side,

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:04.079
a product called
Cloud Data Flow.

00:17:04.079 --> 00:17:05.339
And then they'll make
their way into Spanner.

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:06.547
So let's build this together.

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:11.900
Homework, before
you get started,

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:17.040
is to estimate how much Cloud
Spanner capacity you will use.

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:19.980
Generally, there are some
rules of thumb with regards

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:21.810
to how much storage
should be allocated

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:22.990
per any number of nodes.

00:17:22.990 --> 00:17:26.720
So if you think you'll be
strained on the storage

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:28.470
side of things, you
would need to scale up

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:29.370
the number of nodes.

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:30.950
And so it's basically
a quota system

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:33.390
of two terabytes per node--

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:36.282
two terabytes per node.

00:17:36.282 --> 00:17:37.740
So if you are going
to exceed that,

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:40.110
you just want to
overshoot that to allocate

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:41.310
enough nodes to cover that.

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:42.992
If you're well within
that, then you're

00:17:42.992 --> 00:17:45.450
going to design on the side of
throughput in Spanner, which

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:48.417
is how much transactional
throughput do I need,

00:17:48.417 --> 00:17:49.500
which is the second point.

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:51.240
So now I know how
much storage I have.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:53.742
How much I/O do I
have on this database?

00:17:53.742 --> 00:17:55.200
And then understand
the data model.

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:56.230
Where am I coming from?

00:17:56.230 --> 00:18:00.040
So in the case of a
DynamoDB origination,

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:02.430
Dynamo has a couple
of choices for how its

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:03.600
structures its primary keys.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:06.960
Is it hash or is
it hash plus range?

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:10.560
Like we said in the last slide,
then, across all my items

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:12.930
in my table, what
is the superset

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:15.780
or the set of
collection of attributes

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.180
so that I can understand
how to create my columns?

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:21.420
What are the data types
of those attributes?

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.730
And then, also, keep track
of any secondary indexes.

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:26.354
This sort of, if you do this as
a worksheet or, as like I said,

00:18:26.354 --> 00:18:30.690
as homework, it sets you up
for a nice migration path.

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:32.490
First step is understand
the differences.

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:34.320
The data types are
generally there,

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:35.820
but there could be
some differences.

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:40.200
So understand how data types
from one map to the other.

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.920
Generally, you're going
to find all the same data

00:18:42.920 --> 00:18:44.310
types if not more.

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:51.206
But this is the canonical
mapping of data types.

00:18:51.206 --> 00:18:52.330
So let's make it practical.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:55.990
Let's start with a
baseline DynamoDB table.

00:18:55.990 --> 00:18:57.780
This is a table
you could create.

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:01.550
It's using a simplistic
hash key as its primary key.

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:02.800
It's not using hash and range.

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:05.060
And it's a hash key
called Username.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:07.310
We have a little bit of
provisioned throughput.

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:09.940
And what I'm not
showing on here is

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:13.600
that, in order to facilitate
that streaming hook,

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:16.540
this table would also have
streams enabled, DynamoDB

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:18.419
streams, which would
just be another flag

00:19:18.419 --> 00:19:19.210
on the end of this.

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:20.650
Or you could adjust your table.

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:22.865
If you have a table
that's already running,

00:19:22.865 --> 00:19:26.200
you could simply augment
it and add streams to it.

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:27.940
So let's create the
equivalent table,

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:30.490
sort of taking that metadata we
went through, in the homework

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:32.656
collection, and we're going
to create the equivalent

00:19:32.656 --> 00:19:33.766
table in Cloud Spanner.

00:19:33.766 --> 00:19:35.770
And so let's say that we've
determined-- in that source

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:37.770
table, we've looked at
all the attributes inside

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:40.150
of our DynamoDB table and
decided that, really, there's

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:42.760
just sort of four consistent
attributes or four attributes

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:46.510
that represent the majority
of what we're looking at.

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:49.000
It's like Zipcode,
Subscribed, whether somebody's

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.100
subscribed to a
feed or not, when

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:52.810
they're sent a
reminder date, maybe

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:53.650
some points they've earned.

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:55.816
So this is a fictitious
table, but it's probably not

00:19:55.816 --> 00:19:58.540
too dissimilar from
the way that you

00:19:58.540 --> 00:20:01.300
would use a simple
database, a straightforward

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:05.290
database like DynamoDB that's
more key value oriented.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:09.970
So we'll map that nicely
onto a Cloud Spanner schema.

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:11.904
To get to that point,
in the beginning,

00:20:11.904 --> 00:20:13.570
I alluded, it's
actually really trivial.

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:15.670
This is almost the
entirety of what

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:18.370
you have to get going
with in Cloud Spanner.

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:22.180
Pick where you want it,
how much capacity you want,

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:24.490
and what you want to call it.

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:25.390
If you hit Create--

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:26.890
and I think you we'll
be showing this later.

00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:28.630
If you hit Create,
it's a few seconds.

00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:31.690
In a few seconds you've got that
much capacity sitting there.

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:35.140
And so in a three-node,
regional setup,

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:38.560
this is at roughly 30,000
reads a second, consistent,

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:40.140
with 6,000 writes a second.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:43.150
So that's a lot of capacity,
especially when you're

00:20:43.150 --> 00:20:46.420
talking about maybe dealing with
much lower capacity in terms

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:47.560
of provisioned throughput.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:49.060
Just for three
nodes, you get a lot.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:51.601
And three nodes would be kind
of the minimum for a production

00:20:51.601 --> 00:20:52.984
sized setup.

00:20:52.984 --> 00:20:54.400
There's also
multi-regional, which

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:57.740
implies more quorum copies.

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:00.000
I would encourage you
to go to a Cloud Spanner

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.890
101 talk to understand
how the quorum works,

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.110
and where the writes
are distributed.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:08.230
But basically, if you do
this, within a few seconds,

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:10.810
you've got a tremendous
bucket of input output.

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:13.520
So that table I showed on the
previous screen that said, hey,

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:15.400
we've analyzed our
source table, figured out

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:17.920
how it looks in Dynamo,
figured out the columns,

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:20.380
how do we get the columns to
something database oriented?

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:21.682
So within the instance--

00:21:21.682 --> 00:21:23.140
so in the previous
screen, I showed

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:25.250
how to create an instance--
within that instance,

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:28.190
we can now begin
instantiating databases.

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:29.920
Databases are sort of
logical containers.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:31.900
The instance is the
ubiquitous sea of capacity.

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:34.960
And then the databases
are specific logical units

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:37.234
inside of that database
so you can create tables.

00:21:37.234 --> 00:21:39.400
Looks and feels pretty much
like any other database.

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:41.210
It's really not that dissimilar.

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:45.635
But we take our DDL, which is
standard DDL, plug that in,

00:21:45.635 --> 00:21:48.297
and we create our database.

00:21:48.297 --> 00:21:50.630
One thing that we would do,
at this point, in our table,

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:52.630
is we'd run back to
our Dynamo table.

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:55.570
And using DynamoDB
Streams and Lambda,

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:57.880
we want to capture
all the changes off.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:00.814
And so I would turn around.

00:22:00.814 --> 00:22:02.230
I would make a
function like this,

00:22:02.230 --> 00:22:03.669
a Lambda function like this.

00:22:03.669 --> 00:22:05.710
And this becomes the glue
between all the changes

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:10.030
happening inside of
my DynamoDB table

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:14.260
and getting it over
to Google Cloud side.

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:16.360
And we're using basically
Pub/Sub in the middle.

00:22:16.360 --> 00:22:17.880
So basically, what
I'm saying is,

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:20.200
inside of my Lambda
function, every time

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:23.860
I get a change, I've got my
DynamoDB Streams to configure,

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:25.320
to send me the new image.

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:26.800
With DynamoDB you
can say, send me

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:28.619
the old image, the
new image, or both.

00:22:28.619 --> 00:22:30.910
And so all I'm doing is I'm
taking the entire payload ,

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:32.118
and I'm just shoving it over.

00:22:32.118 --> 00:22:34.270
And this basically
has Lambda make a call

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:35.770
and sends it over to Pub/Sub.

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:39.250
We'll show this in
action in a minute.

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:40.080
So it's in Pub/Sub.

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:44.340
Pub/Sub is our highly scalable,
distributed messaging system,

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:47.130
in which case you can
be pumping messages in,

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:49.650
at a million a second or
something ridiculous, whatever

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:52.710
your application needs, and
have n number of subscribers.

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:55.189
They can be pull subscribers
or push subscribers,

00:22:55.189 --> 00:22:57.480
where something is contacting
and pulling for messages,

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:01.290
or it can push to an endpoint,
any kind of endpoint,

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:02.950
like a Cloud
Function or whatnot.

00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:04.865
We're going to be
using a pull mechanism.

00:23:07.624 --> 00:23:09.040
This would be an
interesting time,

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:12.190
if your application had
the luxury, where you may--

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:14.710
this would be the inflection
point, at which you may decide,

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.560
hey, for consistency's
sake, let's do a quick pause

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.570
while we kick off an export.

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:21.752
It's not strictly
needed depending

00:23:21.752 --> 00:23:23.210
on how much data
you think is going

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:25.900
to be mutated during this
time or overlap of keys.

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:28.454
But this would be that moment
you would decide, hey, we're

00:23:28.454 --> 00:23:29.620
going to kick off an export.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:33.562
Should we pause
writes for a second,

00:23:33.562 --> 00:23:35.770
so that we can keep track
of exactly when this Lambda

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:38.440
function is instantiated and
when the export was kicked off.

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:40.648
It's a momentary thing that
you would have to decide.

00:23:40.648 --> 00:23:42.930
It's not strictly needed.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:43.870
So how do we get data?

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.480
So we've already put
in a Lambda function.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:46.730
That's sending over
the streaming data.

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:48.390
Basically, now, how
do we do the bulk?

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:49.690
So that becomes our
checkpointing time.

00:23:49.690 --> 00:23:50.670
Like we put in this function.

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:51.800
We've started collecting data.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:53.190
It's changing as of this moment.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:56.250
Now, how do we do
the mass export?

00:23:56.250 --> 00:24:00.152
So you need to lean
into the platform that

00:24:00.152 --> 00:24:01.110
hosts that data source.

00:24:01.110 --> 00:24:04.427
In the case of DynamoDB,
there's a tool.

00:24:04.427 --> 00:24:05.760
You can run a data pipeline job.

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:07.470
That is the canonical
way to get data out

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:09.330
if you were to go
and say Export.

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:12.660
But what it does is it lands
that data into an S3 bucket.

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:15.430
That kicks off an EMR process.

00:24:15.430 --> 00:24:17.430
Elastic MapReduce process
reaches in the table

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:20.010
and then kicks out
flat files that

00:24:20.010 --> 00:24:24.007
are JSON files and puts
those into an S3 bucket.

00:24:24.007 --> 00:24:25.840
So if you think back
to the Lambda function,

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:27.240
that Lambda function
was our glue

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:28.680
that got us from
changes happening

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:30.690
in Dynamo over to Pub/Sub.

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:32.800
If you think about
our S3 bucket,

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:35.250
the glue that sort of
moves it from Amazon-side

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:39.800
to the Google-side
is a Copy command.

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:42.300
Before you do the Copy, you
just kind of look at the bucket.

00:24:42.300 --> 00:24:43.980
There should be a
success file inside

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:45.354
of the export that
would tell you

00:24:45.354 --> 00:24:47.800
that everything went correct.

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:49.860
But essentially, you
can use the gsutil--

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:52.110
it's the Google
Storage utility--

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.240
that understands how to read
Amazon credential files.

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:56.922
So this is a very handy feature.

00:24:56.922 --> 00:24:59.130
Essentially, let's say,
you're working on an instance

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:00.360
or you're working
inside a Cloud Shell,

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:02.610
and you've installed the
Amazon command line utilities

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:05.700
and, obviously, now the
Google command line utilities,

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:07.160
including gsutil.

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:10.965
Gsutil knows how to read your
Amazon credentials if they're

00:25:10.965 --> 00:25:12.090
configured on the same box.

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:14.710
And you can literally
pass it an S3 URL.

00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:17.160
So, in this case, I can
say, go from the S3 bucket

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:20.384
to my cloud storage
bucket and rsync.

00:25:20.384 --> 00:25:22.050
So just tell me
everything that's there.

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:24.840
If it's an especially
large transfer,

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:28.006
we have a background operation
called Cloud Storage Transfer

00:25:28.006 --> 00:25:30.172
Service that will do this
for you in the background.

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:36.220
And now both sets of artifacts,
both the Pub/Sub streaming

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:40.370
changes as well as the
flat files from S3,

00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:42.422
have shown up on the
Google side of the fence.

00:25:42.422 --> 00:25:43.880
So what do we do
with those things?

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:48.100
The first thing is we do a job
to import all those flat files

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:48.730
from S3.

00:25:48.730 --> 00:25:51.580
So we would kick
off a Dataflow job.

00:25:51.580 --> 00:25:53.850
And Dataflow is
our managed service

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:55.850
for managing Apache
Beam pipelines.

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:58.714
And so we would have
a pipeline written,

00:25:58.714 --> 00:26:00.130
an Apache Beam
pipeline that knows

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:02.110
how to take flat
files, in JSON format,

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:04.450
and write those into Cloud
Spanner using something

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:07.800
called SpannerIO, which is
a mechanism for Apache Beam

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:09.250
to write to Spanner.

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:11.840
And that would run through,
and that would complete.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:13.954
And the nice thing
about Cloud Data Flows,

00:26:13.954 --> 00:26:15.370
it takes your
Apache Beam pipeline

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:18.760
and then gives you this
very introspective view

00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:21.400
into your pipeline, how it
is and how many messages

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:23.830
are being written, what
was the success factor.

00:26:23.830 --> 00:26:26.560
And so you would monitor the
progress, verify item counts.

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:29.620
When it's done, perhaps do
some selects between the two

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:33.040
databases and make sure that
the item count between one table

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:35.830
and another table are the same.

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:38.710
And do some spot checking.

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:40.472
Look at the item in
one database and look

00:26:40.472 --> 00:26:41.680
at the item in the other one.

00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:43.304
You're going to want
to do this, maybe,

00:26:43.304 --> 00:26:47.274
for different items
inside of your database.

00:26:47.274 --> 00:26:49.690
So now the more interesting
challenge, which is how do you

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:51.106
get those streaming
changes, which

00:26:51.106 --> 00:26:53.500
have been getting into the
Pub/Sub queue, which haven't

00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:55.360
been picked up yet,
how do we get those out

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:57.670
and pushed into Spanner?

00:26:57.670 --> 00:27:04.690
So now we want to create a
subscription to that topic

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:06.370
and run the same thing.

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:08.000
But this is now a streaming job.

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:09.950
This will not terminate
like the other job,

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:10.866
which was a batch job.

00:27:10.866 --> 00:27:13.570
This is a streaming
job that kicks off.

00:27:13.570 --> 00:27:15.274
And it's very similar.

00:27:15.274 --> 00:27:17.440
This is a DAG structure,
which is like, essentially,

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:18.981
a structure of
activities that you've

00:27:18.981 --> 00:27:20.930
asked to happen in a sequence.

00:27:20.930 --> 00:27:24.370
And now the streaming pipeline
will stay and continue them.

00:27:24.370 --> 00:27:26.140
It's not going to
terminate, because you

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:28.540
expect messages to keep coming
in until the migration is

00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:30.127
done, right?

00:27:30.127 --> 00:27:31.960
And what this does,
it's slightly different.

00:27:31.960 --> 00:27:33.500
It's not just new records.

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:37.270
It has to look and see is this
a created or an update and/or

00:27:37.270 --> 00:27:38.230
a delete.

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:39.940
This is because the
Spanner API calls

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:42.010
that do these are
slightly different.

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:43.760
And so you need to
separate this logic.

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:45.430
So I've shown you
some Dataflow code.

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:48.620
I don't want to make
this slide [INAUDIBLE]..

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:50.930
So let's show you some of
the background behind it.

00:27:50.930 --> 00:27:52.429
So let's look at
the batch pipeline.

00:27:52.429 --> 00:27:55.690
It's a little more concise for
the purposes of demonstration.

00:27:55.690 --> 00:27:57.050
But this is Apache Beam code.

00:27:57.050 --> 00:27:59.470
This is a kind of trimmed
down version of the pipeline.

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:01.270
You create these pipelines.

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:03.125
And you essentially
just create these stages

00:28:03.125 --> 00:28:04.000
within your pipeline.

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:05.800
And this is a pretty
straightforward one.

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:07.720
You can annotate it with
a friendly name that

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:09.800
will be represented in the UI.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:10.930
But I want to ReadItems.

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:11.830
I want to ParseItems.

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:13.538
I want to CreateMutations
and WriteItems.

00:28:13.538 --> 00:28:16.600
It sounds pretty-- it sounds
like the canonical activities

00:28:16.600 --> 00:28:18.479
that you would go through.

00:28:18.479 --> 00:28:19.770
And let's look at one of these.

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:21.853
Let's look at like ParseItems,
which is basically,

00:28:21.853 --> 00:28:24.160
how do I take that
in-coming JSON, that's

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:27.760
coming from the records
that are coming from Dynamo,

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:29.906
and how do I map those?

00:28:29.906 --> 00:28:31.780
What is that step that
just said, ParseItems,

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:33.340
in my Apache Beam pipeline?

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:34.840
What that's doing
is it's mapping it

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:36.670
to a Java object in this case.

00:28:36.670 --> 00:28:39.020
And so you would have
some code that just says,

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:43.090
hey, map JSON with Gson.

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:45.670
And now I've got this object
representation of each record

00:28:45.670 --> 00:28:47.249
that's coming through.

00:28:47.249 --> 00:28:49.540
So now that I've got each
record that's coming through,

00:28:49.540 --> 00:28:53.790
the next thing in my pipeline
list was CreateMutations.

00:28:53.790 --> 00:28:57.190
CreateMutations takes each
of these class members

00:28:57.190 --> 00:29:00.320
now and sort of lets me
map them to column names.

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:03.190
This was the second
step in that pipeline.

00:29:03.190 --> 00:29:04.940
And so, once that's
done, then, basically,

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:07.300
that pipeline has what it needs.

00:29:07.300 --> 00:29:10.460
One thing I'll touch on is
the Dynamo, we mentioned,

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:13.180
that could have a
slightly different

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:15.465
primary key structure, which
would be hash and range.

00:29:15.465 --> 00:29:17.590
This is where you would
take advantage of a feature

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:20.440
within Spanner, called
interleaved tables,

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:23.290
where one table can be nested
inside of another table

00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:28.030
by attribution of a
commonly shared primary key

00:29:28.030 --> 00:29:29.814
or the first part
of a primary key.

00:29:29.814 --> 00:29:31.480
So if you notice,
these both share, say,

00:29:31.480 --> 00:29:34.010
Username as a primary
key but also OrderNumber.

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:37.540
This physically co-locates
data that's like.

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:41.050
So if the primary key was
Niel, and then the next thing

00:29:41.050 --> 00:29:42.579
was Niel and his
orders, those would

00:29:42.579 --> 00:29:44.620
be sort of co-located from
a storage perspective.

00:29:44.620 --> 00:29:47.500
They would be operated on by
the same nodes within Spanner.

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:49.570
Or they, technically,
would be in the same split,

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:51.490
so it would be managed
by the same nodes.

00:29:51.490 --> 00:29:54.430
This makes things
like co-located

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:56.847
joins very fast and efficient.

00:29:59.472 --> 00:30:01.180
Also, secondary indexes,
if you remember,

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:02.560
the original interrogation
was, are there

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:04.240
any secondary
indexes, any indexes

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:05.689
on non-primary key fields?

00:30:05.689 --> 00:30:07.480
You would turn around
and, now, at the end,

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:08.847
would be the time to apply that.

00:30:08.847 --> 00:30:10.180
And Spanner does the same thing.

00:30:10.180 --> 00:30:11.900
If you don't have
a secondary index,

00:30:11.900 --> 00:30:14.779
and you ran a query on
an non-primary key field,

00:30:14.779 --> 00:30:17.320
you're essentially going to get
a full table scan coming back

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:17.950
at you.

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:20.440
So we want to create a secondary
index on whatever columns

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:24.020
are going to be
interesting to our query.

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:27.880
So I'll do a quick little
run through on verifying

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:28.850
replication.

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:31.046
This is demo time,
so this is usually

00:30:31.046 --> 00:30:32.170
when the sweat beads start.

00:30:32.170 --> 00:30:37.320
So if we could, I'll
cut through this.

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:39.340
All right.

00:30:39.340 --> 00:30:45.267
So to be respectful of time,
I'm going to just sort of run

00:30:45.267 --> 00:30:46.100
through some trivia.

00:30:46.100 --> 00:30:47.572
I'm going to create three keys.

00:30:47.572 --> 00:30:49.780
I'm going to deal with three
keys in my Dynamo table.

00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:52.520
I'm going to have
my-add-key, my-change-key

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:53.660
and my-delete-key.

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:55.160
So the add one, I'm
going to add in,

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:56.659
see if it comes over to Spanner.

00:30:56.659 --> 00:30:58.700
The change one, I'm going
to change an attribute.

00:30:58.700 --> 00:30:59.840
And the delete one, I'm
going to get rid of.

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:01.340
I'm going to basically
see those happen,

00:31:01.340 --> 00:31:03.298
and then walk through
everything that happened.

00:31:03.298 --> 00:31:05.010
So let's just
verify a few things.

00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:09.089
Number one is I
don't have a key.

00:31:09.089 --> 00:31:10.130
I don't have the add key.

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:12.960
That's the one I'm
about to add in.

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:16.336
Let's look at the key
I'm going to change.

00:31:16.336 --> 00:31:17.960
The key I'm going to
change, right now,

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:19.860
is currently set to False.

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:22.490
And so anybody want to
take a guess at what

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:24.270
I'm going to change it to?

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:25.160
True, all right.

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:32.190
And then lastly, I'm
going to run Delete,

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:35.910
and so there's a record there
that we're going to wipe away.

00:31:35.910 --> 00:31:39.140
So let's go and mess with
these things on Dynamo-side.

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:42.060
So I'm in my Dynamo
table, and the first thing

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:46.280
is I'm going to
just add my-add-key,

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:53.800
and I'm going to add a
Boolean called Subscribed.

00:31:53.800 --> 00:31:56.170
And I'm going to call it False.

00:31:56.170 --> 00:31:58.530
So let's add that.

00:31:58.530 --> 00:32:00.325
And I'm going to
also then modify--

