00:39:20.940 --> 00:39:23.610
So as Sami talked
about NoSQL, and I'm

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:25.360
going to have to run
through this quickly,

00:39:25.360 --> 00:39:27.870
because we've got less time
than we thought we had.

00:39:27.870 --> 00:39:28.940
Sami talked about NoSQL.

00:39:28.940 --> 00:39:32.420
I'm going to be talking about
the relational database side

00:39:32.420 --> 00:39:34.350
import.

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:36.360
My examples would
be MySQL, specific,

00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:40.030
but the general case applies
to most relational databases.

00:39:40.030 --> 00:39:43.150
So Sami's already
talked about why.

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:45.510
The advantages are
reasonably obvious,

00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:47.550
no planned downtime, no ops.

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:48.682
Everything is managed view.

00:39:48.682 --> 00:39:50.640
Operating system, hardware
and software updates

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:51.810
happen all in the background.

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:53.935
Horizontally scaled, so
you can just add more power

00:39:53.935 --> 00:39:54.790
when you want to.

00:39:54.790 --> 00:39:55.470
No sharding.

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:57.011
You've got a globally
consistent view

00:39:57.011 --> 00:39:59.610
of your data and
external consistency,

00:39:59.610 --> 00:40:01.920
with ACID-compliant
write transactions.

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:04.570
Read transactions
allow you to read data

00:40:04.570 --> 00:40:07.380
at a particular timestamp,
up to an hour old.

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:11.466
And you've also got
ANSI-2011 SQL queries.

00:40:11.466 --> 00:40:12.840
So the first thing
you want to do

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:16.410
is look at your existing schema
and check the data types.

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:18.840
Spanner has a very
concise set of data types.

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:21.305
There's just this
little bunch here.

00:40:21.305 --> 00:40:22.680
All of these can
be actually used

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:25.250
as keys apart from
the ARRAY type.

00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:27.480
The ARRAY type can be of
any of the other types.

00:40:27.480 --> 00:40:29.910
So you want to look at
your existing schema

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:33.360
and map the existing data
types to the Cloud Spanner data

00:40:33.360 --> 00:40:34.410
types.

00:40:34.410 --> 00:40:36.754
Many of them have
a direct mapping.

00:40:36.754 --> 00:40:38.670
But some of them, as you
can see in this list,

00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:40.260
do not have a direct mapping.

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:43.290
In this case, you want to
change your application database

00:40:43.290 --> 00:40:45.510
access layer, like
we mentioned earlier,

00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:47.760
to convert from your
internal representation

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:49.840
to an equivalent
Spanner representation.

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:52.800
So, for instance, an ENUM, you'd
write to Spanner as a string,

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:56.900
and you'd pass it back
out in your application.

00:40:56.900 --> 00:40:59.942
Table keys are important
consideration in Spanner.

00:40:59.942 --> 00:41:01.400
In traditional
databases, you often

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:06.592
work with sequences, where
your rows increase constantly.

00:41:06.592 --> 00:41:08.050
That is an anti-pattern
in Spanner,

00:41:08.050 --> 00:41:14.990
because Spanner splits the data
into multiple splits, sorted

00:41:14.990 --> 00:41:18.580
by a primary key.

00:41:18.580 --> 00:41:22.090
And when you add data,
it's added to the split.

00:41:22.090 --> 00:41:24.640
So if you've got a sequential
key, always increasing,

00:41:24.640 --> 00:41:27.174
it will always be added
to the last split.

00:41:27.174 --> 00:41:29.340
And if you're doing that a
lot, then you're last bit

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:31.798
becomes the only split where
there's any database activity.

00:41:31.798 --> 00:41:33.101
It becomes a hotspot.

00:41:33.101 --> 00:41:34.850
And you may have 20
nodes, but you're only

00:41:34.850 --> 00:41:36.452
using one to write your data.

00:41:36.452 --> 00:41:37.660
So what do you do about this.

00:41:37.660 --> 00:41:40.740
Well, you need to prefix
your keys with another key

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:42.690
in order to distribute
the data better.

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:45.630
Now, if it is a
greenfield database,

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:49.930
you'd probably use a
unique UID or a random key.

00:41:49.930 --> 00:41:51.660
But because we're
importing, we want

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:53.100
to keep this sequential key.

00:41:53.100 --> 00:41:54.210
So what do we do?

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:56.530
Well, we add a hash.

00:41:56.530 --> 00:41:59.880
We simply do a simple
hash of the original key

00:41:59.880 --> 00:42:02.044
and prefix that in the
Spanner primary key.

00:42:02.044 --> 00:42:03.960
So you see, on the second
row, the primary key

00:42:03.960 --> 00:42:06.890
has a hashed ID, which, in
this case, of just a CRC32.

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:08.100
It doesn't have to be secure.

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:10.860
It just has to be somewhat
random but deterministic,

00:42:10.860 --> 00:42:13.400
based on the original ID.

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:15.850
There is lots of documentation,
on Cloud.Google.com,

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:19.289
about how to hash
keys and other details

00:42:19.289 --> 00:42:21.080
in which to avoid
hotspots in Cloud Spanner

00:42:21.080 --> 00:42:24.770
by using this feature.

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:27.170
Of course, if you
have referential keys,

00:42:27.170 --> 00:42:29.570
you need to include the
hash key in the reference.

00:42:29.570 --> 00:42:32.170
Because it's the primary
key of the existing table,

00:42:32.170 --> 00:42:33.980
so you need to
include the hashed key

00:42:33.980 --> 00:42:36.680
in the referenced table.

00:42:36.680 --> 00:42:40.670
This is to avoid
a full-scale table

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:42.890
scan, when you are
wanting to look up

00:42:42.890 --> 00:42:45.450
the keys, when you do a join.

00:42:45.450 --> 00:42:47.520
Once the application
has been fully migrated,

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:49.020
you can do another
migration, if you want to,

00:42:49.020 --> 00:42:50.520
which is to remove
all the sequences

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:53.590
and switch everything
to unique UIDs.

00:42:53.590 --> 00:42:55.630
Secondary indexes
in Spanner are also

00:42:55.630 --> 00:42:58.090
implemented by tables
under the hood.

00:42:58.090 --> 00:43:01.500
So the exact same problem
occurs with columns

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:02.830
that you've got in an index.

00:43:02.830 --> 00:43:06.160
In this case, we're indexing
events by timestamp.

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:08.574
Timestamp, of course, is a
continuously increasing key.

00:43:08.574 --> 00:43:10.240
And you'll get a
hotspot, because you're

00:43:10.240 --> 00:43:12.680
adding more and more
events, increasing in time,

00:43:12.680 --> 00:43:14.230
so you're getting a hotspot.

00:43:14.230 --> 00:43:15.460
What you do here?

00:43:15.460 --> 00:43:19.150
Well, you do something called
application level sharding,

00:43:19.150 --> 00:43:21.550
which is creating a shard ID.

00:43:21.550 --> 00:43:23.050
This is not the
same as a split ID.

00:43:23.050 --> 00:43:27.760
It's just an application-defined
value to mix up the data a bit.

00:43:27.760 --> 00:43:30.560
In this case, I'm using
the CRC32 of the timestamp,

00:43:30.560 --> 00:43:32.460
module at 100, which
gives you a number

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:35.800
from 0 to 99, which is
relatively random based

00:43:35.800 --> 00:43:37.600
on the timestamp.

00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:40.170
I add this into the table,
and I index on that timestamp

00:43:40.170 --> 00:43:42.910
shard ID, so that way
the index, itself,

00:43:42.910 --> 00:43:44.825
is randomly distributed.

00:43:44.825 --> 00:43:47.200
If we want to SELECT from the
index using a range select,

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:49.780
I have to include
all the shards.

00:43:49.780 --> 00:43:52.300
Because, otherwise, again,
because it's the key,

00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:53.960
Spanner will do a table scan.

00:43:53.960 --> 00:43:57.880
So again, I'm selecting, using
the BETWEEN clause there,

00:43:57.880 --> 00:44:00.400
shards 0 to 99, and then
using the range clause.

00:44:00.400 --> 00:44:04.360
And that gives a limited SELECT
and not a full table scan.

00:44:04.360 --> 00:44:07.205
Sequences do not exist in Cloud
Spanner, because, as I said,

00:44:07.205 --> 00:44:08.080
it's an anti-pattern.

00:44:08.080 --> 00:44:09.880
So for that, you have
to implement them

00:44:09.880 --> 00:44:10.992
in your application.

00:44:10.992 --> 00:44:13.450
The simplest way is to have a
table in your database called

00:44:13.450 --> 00:44:17.862
Sequences, with the sequence
name and the next identifier.

00:44:17.862 --> 00:44:19.570
When you want to add
a row to your table,

00:44:19.570 --> 00:44:21.220
you get the next ID.

00:44:21.220 --> 00:44:23.410
You increment it and update
your Sequences table,

00:44:23.410 --> 00:44:27.250
and then you insert your new row
with, obviously, the hashed ID

00:44:27.250 --> 00:44:29.750
and the new sequence number.

00:44:29.750 --> 00:44:32.540
This is fine for a
rarely modified table.

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:35.020
But if you've got
something happening a lot,

00:44:35.020 --> 00:44:37.300
this, of course,
becomes a choke-point.

00:44:37.300 --> 00:44:41.110
Every insert will have to
wait on the Sequences table

00:44:41.110 --> 00:44:43.789
to do the update.

00:44:43.789 --> 00:44:45.580
We've gone a bit too
far for myself, there.

00:44:45.580 --> 00:44:47.290
So the way to
avoid that would be

00:44:47.290 --> 00:44:52.150
to get each application instance
to manage a block of sequences,

00:44:52.150 --> 00:44:54.640
so to read, say, 100
sequences from the table,

00:44:54.640 --> 00:44:58.280
manage them internally, issuing
the sequence IDs to itself.

00:44:58.280 --> 00:45:00.530
It means you'll lose
the continuous value

00:45:00.530 --> 00:45:02.030
of the sequence to
a certain degree.

00:45:02.030 --> 00:45:05.335
But, as I said, continuous
keys are not the best plan

00:45:05.335 --> 00:45:07.540
in Spanner, in any case.

00:45:07.540 --> 00:45:09.480
Foreign key constraints--

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:12.310
Spanner gives a lot
performance benefits

00:45:12.310 --> 00:45:15.550
by trusting the application
to keep the data consistent.

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:18.850
And one of these things is not
having foreign key constraints

00:45:18.850 --> 00:45:21.010
in other tables.

00:45:21.010 --> 00:45:23.496
This, you can have to
implement in the application

00:45:23.496 --> 00:45:24.120
if you want it.

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:26.140
But in most
applications, it already

00:45:26.140 --> 00:45:29.730
knows what data is there,
what data isn't there.

00:45:29.730 --> 00:45:31.470
And so you wouldn't
be able to create--

00:45:31.470 --> 00:45:33.261
the application shouldn't
be able to create

00:45:33.261 --> 00:45:36.320
an object without the parent
object already existing.

00:45:36.320 --> 00:45:39.190
If you do want to add
foreign key constraints,

00:45:39.190 --> 00:45:41.796
you can easily do it yourself,
in the database access layer,

00:45:41.796 --> 00:45:44.170
simply by checking for the
existence of a reference table

00:45:44.170 --> 00:45:45.230
key.

00:45:45.230 --> 00:45:47.660
And if it doesn't exist,
throw an exception.

00:45:47.660 --> 00:45:49.690
And if it does, then
you're insert your row,

00:45:49.690 --> 00:45:51.439
and there's a foreign
key constraint check

00:45:51.439 --> 00:45:53.580
implemented application side.

00:45:53.580 --> 00:45:55.630
Similarly, ON DELETE
and ON UPDATE rules

00:45:55.630 --> 00:45:58.900
must also be implemented
in the application,

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:01.180
because there's no
foreign key constraints.

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:04.870
You find all the child IDs that
are referenced by the parent.

00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:06.460
You delete them all.

00:46:06.460 --> 00:46:08.567
And then you delete your parent.

00:46:08.567 --> 00:46:10.400
That's an ON Delete
CASCADE rule, of course.

00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:12.330
ON DELETE UPDATE
or ON DELETE set

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:16.360
Null would be implemented
by updating the rows

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:21.080
or abandoned in transaction
should any child rows be there.

00:46:21.080 --> 00:46:24.400
As Sami already mentioned,
the interleaved parent-child

00:46:24.400 --> 00:46:27.460
relationship does have
referential integrity

00:46:27.460 --> 00:46:31.520
constraints and ON DELETE rules.

00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:33.280
So here, we have a
parent and a child.

00:46:33.280 --> 00:46:34.505
We've told it to
interleave in the parents.

00:46:34.505 --> 00:46:36.760
ON DELETE CASCADE, Sami's
mentioned, in detail,

00:46:36.760 --> 00:46:37.300
about that.

00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:38.774
I'm going to just skip over it.

00:46:38.774 --> 00:46:40.940
But in this case, there is
a foreign key constraint.

00:46:40.940 --> 00:46:42.880
And the child table
key must reference

00:46:42.880 --> 00:46:43.797
the key of the parent.

00:46:43.797 --> 00:46:46.213
And in an ON DELETE CASCADE
rule, it'll delete the parent,

00:46:46.213 --> 00:46:47.230
it deletes the children.

00:46:47.230 --> 00:46:49.060
You can create a
hierarchy of six levels

00:46:49.060 --> 00:46:50.350
of tables in this manner.

00:46:50.350 --> 00:46:52.060
But be aware,
because all the child

00:46:52.060 --> 00:46:55.100
tables have to be in the same
split as a top level row.

00:46:55.100 --> 00:46:57.250
The four gigabyte
size split limit

00:46:57.250 --> 00:47:01.487
applies to the size of the top
level row and all its children.

00:47:01.487 --> 00:47:03.070
That hasn't happened
very often, but I

00:47:03.070 --> 00:47:06.560
have had a customer that thought
they would meet that problem.

00:47:06.560 --> 00:47:07.930
Commit timestamps I mentioned.

00:47:07.930 --> 00:47:10.670
You define them in the schema
with an options clause.

00:47:10.670 --> 00:47:13.340
In the application,
you use a special value

00:47:13.340 --> 00:47:15.800
to insert the commit
timestamp, and then you

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:17.570
can use that during
the migration process

00:47:17.570 --> 00:47:20.140
to detect whether a row
has later or earlier data.

00:47:20.140 --> 00:47:21.890
I'm really rushing
through this, because I

00:47:21.890 --> 00:47:25.756
can see the clock counting
down quite rapidly.

00:47:25.756 --> 00:47:27.380
Other things that
you'd have to monitor

00:47:27.380 --> 00:47:28.910
are on the application side.

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:34.670
Default values, easy to put
in your database access layer.

00:47:34.670 --> 00:47:37.800
Stored procedures
and triggers-- well,

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:39.860
do to the distributed
architecture,

00:47:39.860 --> 00:47:43.010
trigger is bit of an
anti-pattern you can easily

00:47:43.010 --> 00:47:44.330
implement in your application.

00:47:44.330 --> 00:47:46.700
And because Spanner
is effectively

00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:50.174
infinitely scalable,
you don't have

00:47:50.174 --> 00:47:52.340
a worry about running
triggers and stored procedures

00:47:52.340 --> 00:47:53.040
in the database.

00:47:53.040 --> 00:47:56.859
You can just have more nodes,
and everything runs better.

00:47:56.859 --> 00:47:59.150
It also gives you the benefit
that the stored procedure

00:47:59.150 --> 00:48:01.100
and triggers you put
in your business logic,

00:48:01.100 --> 00:48:03.270
in the application, with
the rest of the code.

00:48:03.270 --> 00:48:05.420
And you can have it written
in the same language.

00:48:05.420 --> 00:48:09.890
You can unit test it without
needing a test database

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:11.740
instance.

00:48:11.740 --> 00:48:13.910
Per table access privileges
don't exist in Spanner.

00:48:13.910 --> 00:48:17.120
Your access control
is read or write

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:19.050
using the Cloud
Platform IAM controls

00:48:19.050 --> 00:48:22.580
for the whole database.

00:48:22.580 --> 00:48:25.030
And finally-- sorry,
whoop, go back--

00:48:25.030 --> 00:48:25.870
JDBC.

00:48:25.870 --> 00:48:27.670
It's currently read-only.

00:48:27.670 --> 00:48:30.160
And there is no data
modification language

00:48:30.160 --> 00:48:30.940
currently.

00:48:30.940 --> 00:48:34.210
But right at this moment,
there is a second Spanner talk

00:48:34.210 --> 00:48:37.510
going on, over in Moscone South,
where Deepti, our Cloud Spanner

00:48:37.510 --> 00:48:40.270
PM, is announcing that data
modification language is

00:48:40.270 --> 00:48:42.340
being previewed right now.

00:48:42.340 --> 00:48:47.370
And there'll be a read/write
JDBC wrapper coming soon.

00:48:47.370 --> 00:48:50.550
We got a minute or so left, so
let's have a look at my demo

00:48:50.550 --> 00:48:53.490
and see if everything's run OK.

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:56.010
So back to the Dataflow
view, what have we got?

00:48:56.010 --> 00:48:59.460
So we've read 234 source files.

00:48:59.460 --> 00:49:03.810
From that, we've got
20 million records.

00:49:03.810 --> 00:49:08.400
We're adding to Spanner at
the rate of 31,000 per second,

00:49:08.400 --> 00:49:10.230
batching them, of course.

00:49:10.230 --> 00:49:12.585
Down to 300 batches
per second or so.

00:49:12.585 --> 00:49:14.460
And let's have a look
at the logged failures.

00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:18.780
There have been no failures
and 21 million successes.

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:21.150
And just to show that
this is for real,

00:49:21.150 --> 00:49:25.050
I'm going to go, stop this
job, give my database a rest.

00:49:25.050 --> 00:49:25.930
Where are we?

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:28.220
Spanner.

00:49:28.220 --> 00:49:32.020
Let's have a look at
the artist credit table.

00:49:32.020 --> 00:49:35.050
And let's have a look
at some of the data.

00:49:35.050 --> 00:49:38.125
So here, when the data
finally comes out--

00:49:38.125 --> 00:49:38.750
hello, Spanner.

00:49:41.974 --> 00:49:43.390
The demigods are
not smiling on me

00:49:43.390 --> 00:49:46.000
with 20 seconds counting
down on the clock.

00:49:46.000 --> 00:49:47.420
There we go.

00:49:47.420 --> 00:49:51.550
So here, we see that
the data has been added.

00:49:51.550 --> 00:49:55.900
The original, sequential IDs
have been prefixed by a hash

00:49:55.900 --> 00:49:59.830
and, therefore, being completely
mixed up in a random order.

00:49:59.830 --> 00:50:02.580
And if I just want to
run a query on that data,

00:50:02.580 --> 00:50:06.730
we'll run a simple query
selecting all the recordings

00:50:06.730 --> 00:50:08.380
that have the word
cloud in their name,

00:50:08.380 --> 00:50:11.300
joining them with the artist,
and see what we get out.

00:50:11.300 --> 00:50:13.221
Query, bum, bum, bum--

00:50:18.220 --> 00:50:18.720
there we go.

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:20.530
Let's run this query.

00:50:20.530 --> 00:50:22.120
This, when I tested
it yesterday,

00:50:22.120 --> 00:50:23.120
took about five seconds.

00:50:23.120 --> 00:50:24.536
Because it's a
demo, it's probably

00:50:24.536 --> 00:50:26.110
going to take 10
times as long, and I

00:50:26.110 --> 00:50:28.484
could be sitting here talking
to myself for five minutes.

00:50:33.450 --> 00:50:33.950
Yeah.

00:50:37.560 --> 00:50:39.840
So once the query comes
back, having scanned

00:50:39.840 --> 00:50:42.330
its several million
rows, because, of course,

00:50:42.330 --> 00:50:48.090
I am not using an index key, I'm
using a table scan on the name.

00:50:48.090 --> 00:50:51.090
What we'll do is we'll let that
run, because the demo gods are

00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:52.765
obviously not smiling
on me, but run

00:50:52.765 --> 00:50:54.390
through a quick, last
couple of slides,

00:50:54.390 --> 00:50:55.556
and we'll come back to this.

00:50:58.050 --> 00:50:59.490
Other things to wrap up?

00:50:59.490 --> 00:51:03.294
Tomorrow, there's talk on
how Nest Camera service has

00:51:03.294 --> 00:51:04.210
